### What is schema evolution?

Schema evolution is managing changes to data structure over time without breaking existing producers or consumers. Producers and consumers deploy independently, so a producer might start writing a new field before all consumers understand it, or an old consumer might read new data. Without discipline, this breaks pipelines.

```java
package com.bank.schema;

public class SchemaEvolutionOverview {
    // Version 1: original transaction event
    record TxnV1(String txnId, String accountId,
                 java.math.BigDecimal amount) {}

    // Version 2: added 'channel' field
    record TxnV2(String txnId, String accountId,
                 java.math.BigDecimal amount,
                 String channel) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // V1 data still readable by V2 consumer
        // (channel defaults to null)
        TxnV1 old = new TxnV1("T1", "SAV2001",
            new java.math.BigDecimal("15000"));
        System.out.println("V1 event: " + old);

        TxnV2 newer = new TxnV2("T2", "SAV2001",
            new java.math.BigDecimal("25000"), "MOBILE");
        System.out.println("V2 event: " + newer);
    }
}
```

```text
V1 event: TxnV1[txnId=T1, accountId=SAV2001, amount=15000]
V2 event: TxnV2[txnId=T2, accountId=SAV2001, amount=25000, channel=MOBILE]
```

### How does a Schema Registry work?

A Schema Registry is the central service that stores every version of each schema and enforces compatibility rules when a producer tries to register a new version. The registry rejects changes that would break consumers.

```java
package com.bank.schema;

public class RegistryRules {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Compatibility modes:");
        System.out.println("  BACKWARD: new schema reads old data");
        System.out.println("    -> add fields WITH defaults");
        System.out.println("  FORWARD: old schema reads new data");
        System.out.println("    -> remove optional fields only");
        System.out.println("  FULL: both directions");
        System.out.println("    -> add/remove with defaults only");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Registry ACCEPTS:");
        System.out.println("  Add nullable 'channel' with default");
        System.out.println("Registry REJECTS:");
        System.out.println("  Add mandatory 'channel' (no default)");
        System.out.println("  Rename 'amount' to 'txn_amount'");
        System.out.println("  Change 'amount' from decimal to string");
    }
}
```

```text
Compatibility modes:
  BACKWARD: new schema reads old data
    -> add fields WITH defaults
  FORWARD: old schema reads new data
    -> remove optional fields only
  FULL: both directions
    -> add/remove with defaults only

Registry ACCEPTS:
  Add nullable 'channel' with default
Registry REJECTS:
  Add mandatory 'channel' (no default)
  Rename 'amount' to 'txn_amount'
  Change 'amount' from decimal to string
```

### How do you wire schema evolution into a Kafka pipeline?

Producers serialize with the registry: the serializer fetches or registers the schema id, and each message carries only the schema id rather than the full schema. Consumers fetch the matching schema to deserialize. This keeps messages small and guarantees both sides agree.

```java
package com.bank.schema;

import java.util.Properties;

public class KafkaSchemaWiring {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties producerProps = new Properties();
        producerProps.put("schema.registry.url",
            "http://registry:8081");
        producerProps.put("key.serializer",
            "io.confluent.kafka.serializers"
            + ".KafkaAvroSerializer");
        producerProps.put("value.serializer",
            "io.confluent.kafka.serializers"
            + ".KafkaAvroSerializer");

        System.out.println("Producer serializes with registry:");
        System.out.println("  Message = [schema_id (4 bytes)]"
            + " + [Avro payload]");
        System.out.println("  Schema stored centrally, not per msg");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Consumer deserializes:");
        System.out.println("  Reads schema_id -> fetches schema"
            + " from registry -> deserializes payload");
    }
}
```

```text
Producer serializes with registry:
  Message = [schema_id (4 bytes)] + [Avro payload]
  Schema stored centrally, not per msg

Consumer deserializes:
  Reads schema_id -> fetches schema from registry -> deserializes payload
```

### Why do columnar formats matter for analytics?

Columnar formats like Parquet store values column by column instead of row by row. This layout enables three major performance wins for analytical queries.

```java
package com.bank.schema;

public class ColumnarBenefits {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Column pruning:");
        System.out.println("   Query needs only 'amount' from"
            + " 100-column table.");
        System.out.println("   Parquet reads only amount column.");
        System.out.println("   Row-store reads all 100 columns.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("2. Predicate pushdown:");
        System.out.println("   Filter: amount > 100000");
        System.out.println("   Row group max = 50000 -> SKIP");
        System.out.println("   Row group max = 200000 -> READ");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3. Compression:");
        System.out.println("   Same-type values compress 5-10x.");
        System.out.println("   Dictionary encoding for branches.");
        System.out.println("   Run-length encoding for dates.");
    }
}
```

```text
1. Column pruning:
   Query needs only 'amount' from 100-column table.
   Parquet reads only amount column.
   Row-store reads all 100 columns.

2. Predicate pushdown:
   Filter: amount > 100000
   Row group max = 50000 -> SKIP
   Row group max = 200000 -> READ

3. Compression:
   Same-type values compress 5-10x.
   Dictionary encoding for branches.
   Run-length encoding for dates.
```

### How does column pruning work?

Column pruning means reading only the columns a query needs. On a 100-column transaction table, if the query only selects amount and date, Parquet reads just those two columns' bytes and never touches the other 98, cutting I/O by orders of magnitude.

```python
# Column pruning in action
# Table has 100 columns, query needs only 2
df = spark.read.parquet(
    "s3://bank-lake/curated/transactions/")

# This reads ONLY amount and txn_date columns
result = df.select("amount", "txn_date") \
    .groupBy("txn_date") \
    .sum("amount")

result.explain(True)  # Physical plan shows column pruning
```

```text
== Physical Plan ==
*(1) HashAggregate(keys=[txn_date], functions=[sum(amount)])
+- FileScan parquet [amount, txn_date]   <-- only 2 of 100 columns read
   PushedFilters: []
   ReadSchema: struct<amount:decimal(15,2),txn_date:string>
```

### How does predicate pushdown work?

Predicate pushdown uses per-row-group min/max statistics to skip irrelevant data. When Spark pushes a filter like `amount > 100000`, the reader checks each row group's max for amount; if the max is below the threshold, the entire row group is skipped without decompression.

```python
# Predicate pushdown with Parquet statistics
df = spark.read.parquet(
    "s3://bank-lake/curated/transactions/")

# Filter pushed to Parquet reader
high_value = df.filter("amount > 100000")
high_value.explain(True)
```

```text
== Physical Plan ==
*(1) Filter (amount > 100000)
+- FileScan parquet [txn_id, amount, ...]
   PushedFilters: [GreaterThan(amount, 100000)]
   ReadSchema: struct<txn_id:string, amount:decimal(15,2)>
   -- Row groups with max(amount) < 100000 are SKIPPED
```

### Why does sort order matter for pushdown?

Min/max statistics only help if the per-block ranges are tight. If data is sorted by amount, block zero holds 0-10000, block one holds 10000-20000, so a filter cleanly isolates blocks. If unsorted, every block holds a random spread and no block is ever skipped.

```java
package com.bank.schema;

public class SortOrderMatters {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Sorted by amount:");
        System.out.println("  Block 0: min=0, max=10000");
        System.out.println("  Block 1: min=10001, max=20000");
        System.out.println("  Block 2: min=20001, max=50000");
        System.out.println("  Filter: amount > 30000");
        System.out.println("  -> Blocks 0,1 SKIPPED. Block 2 READ.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Unsorted:");
        System.out.println("  Block 0: min=50, max=49000");
        System.out.println("  Block 1: min=100, max=48000");
        System.out.println("  Block 2: min=75, max=50000");
        System.out.println("  Filter: amount > 30000");
        System.out.println("  -> ALL blocks must be read."
            + " Statistics useless.");
    }
}
```

```text
Sorted by amount:
  Block 0: min=0, max=10000
  Block 1: min=10001, max=20000
  Block 2: min=20001, max=50000
  Filter: amount > 30000
  -> Blocks 0,1 SKIPPED. Block 2 READ.

Unsorted:
  Block 0: min=50, max=49000
  Block 1: min=100, max=48000
  Block 2: min=75, max=50000
  Filter: amount > 30000
  -> ALL blocks must be read. Statistics useless.
```

